def methods2():
    b = []
    p = lambda s: b.append(('p', s))
    h2 = lambda s: b.append(('h2', s))
    rw, sf, nc = (S2['response_weighting'], S2['supplement_facts'], S2['numerical_checks'])
    b.append(('h1', 'Supplementary Methods: Study 2 (NHANES 2009–2010 and 2001–2002)'))
    h2('Provenance')
    p('Study 2 was analysed before Study 1 as a separate analysis of urine specimens and is reported here without reanalysis. Every value comes from its locked result files; a collection script asserts them against the previously reviewed values. Processed participant data and code for the primary, sensitivity, 2001–2002, flag, selection and fasting analyses are archived (version 2.0.0);[[paper1]] the package recalculates from processed inputs and excludes raw-data preparation and figure code, and the blood pressure benchmark is not part of it.')
    h2('Cohort construction and predictor coding')
    p('Participants were linked by SEQN within each cycle; demographic and laboratory files were checked for unique records, and prescription records were reduced to participant-level statin status. The 2009–2010 cohort used the random and first-morning UACR released in ALB_CR_F;[[nh_albcr_f]] the 2001–2002 sample linked the baseline L16_B specimen to the repeat L16_2_B specimen.[[nh_l16_2_b]] Sequential eligibility is shown in Supplementary Table S17, source files in Supplementary Table S25 and input completeness in Supplementary Table S26.')
    p("The five exclusion histories (congestive heart failure, coronary heart disease, angina, myocardial infarction and stroke; MCQ160B–F) each had to be explicitly answered no. Current smoking required at least 100 lifetime cigarettes and smoking every day or some days. SBP was the mean of the available positive readings (BPXSY1–4), and antihypertensive treatment came from the medication questions with their documented skips. Diabetes was present with a reported diagnosis, insulin, diabetes pills or HbA1c ≥6.5%; a negative classification required DIQ010 in {2, 3}, HbA1c <6.5% and DIQ050 = 2, together with DIQ070 = 2 or the documented skip (2009–2010: DIQ070 missing with DIQ010 = 2 and DIQ160 in {2, 7, 9}; 2001–2002: DIQ070 missing under that cycle's skip).[[nh_diq_f,nh_diq_b]] Missing HbA1c did not exclude a participant whose diabetes was already established. Statin use came from prescription ingredients; unresolved potential lipid-lowering combinations remained unknown, and the 2001–2002 dictionary included cerivastatin. Disclosed pregnancy (RIDEXPRG = 1) was excluded; indeterminate status was retained and excluded in a sensitivity analysis. The public pregnancy release covered women aged 20–44 years in 2009–2010 and 8–59 years in 2001–2002,[[nh_demo_f,nh_demo_b]] and missing status outside these ranges was not treated as confirmed nonpregnancy.")
    p('Inputs were required to lie within total cholesterol 130–320 mg/dL, HDL cholesterol 20–100 mg/dL, SBP 90–200 mm Hg, eGFR 15–140 mL/min/1.73 m² and UACR 0.1–25,000 mg/g; no value was clamped. UACR (mg/g) was 100 × urine albumin (µg/mL)/urine creatinine (mg/dL), and released 2009–2010 ratios agreed with this arithmetic within rounding. Eight final 2009–2010 pairs had released albumin below 0.3 µg/mL; their released values were kept and excluded in a sensitivity analysis. Serum creatinine was LBXSCR (2009–2010) and LBDSCR (2001–2002); neither needed the calibration applied to NHANES III.[[selvin2007]] eGFR used the 2021 CKD-EPI creatinine equation.[[inker2021]] Body mass index was not required because its coefficients in this equation are zero.')
    h2('2001–2002 urine creatinine bridge')
    p('The 2001–2002 specimens used the earlier urine creatinine method. The NCHS bridge[[nh_albcr_e]] was applied to the native value X (mg/dL) of each specimen before UACR was calculated: (1.02√X − 0.36)² for X < 75, (1.05√X − 0.74)² for 75 ≤ X < 250 and (1.01√X − 0.10)² for X ≥ 250, with the branch selected on native X. Albumin and serum creatinine were not transformed, uncertainty in the bridge coefficients was not propagated, and native-assay scoring was a prespecified sensitivity analysis in the same participants.')
    h2('Survey estimation and response weighting')
    p(f"For domain indicator D, weight w and measure y, the estimate was Σ(wDy)/Σ(wD); linearized contributions were summed within primary sampling units (PSUs), and the variance within a stratum with m PSUs was m/(m − 1) times the sum of squared deviations of PSU totals from their mean. Proportions used logit-scale t intervals ({S2['design']['df']} degrees of freedom) and means linear intervals. The 2001–2002 sample used equal weights with its 15 strata and 30 PSUs (15 degrees of freedom); these intervals are conditional on the selected sample and imply no national inference. The response-model target was the {rw['target_n']:,} otherwise-eligible participants, of whom {rw['responders_n']:,} had an eligible second UACR. Covariates were centred age and age squared, sex, smoking, diabetes, antihypertensive treatment, statin use, eGFR, SBP, non-HDL and HDL cholesterol and log random UACR, with examination weights scaled to their mean. Responders received the examination weight divided by the fitted response probability, and every delete-one-PSU replicate refitted the model (jackknife factor (m − 1)/m). Fitted probabilities ranged from {sf['response_probability_range'][0]} to {sf['response_probability_range'][1]}, the largest inverse-response multiplier was {rw['maximum_inverse_response_multiplier']:.2f}, and the largest absolute standardized covariate difference between responders and the target fell from {sf['max_abs_smd_before_after'][0]:.3f} to {sf['max_abs_smd_before_after'][1]:.3f} after weighting. Extreme bounds assigned all {rw['nonresponders_n']} missing second-specimen outcomes first to no change and then to change; they are not confidence intervals.")
    h2('Amendment and numerical verification')
    p(f"Eligibility was finalized after a pilot: the amendment removed unused body mass index restrictions, allowed missing HbA1c when diabetes was established, extended the SBP range to 200 mm Hg, refined unknown medication coding and excluded disclosed pregnancy. The original pilot had {sf['pilot']['n']:,} participants and {sf['pilot']['events']} changes (weighted {F(sf['pilot']['pct'])}%); applying the coding and pregnancy corrections within it left {sf['pilot_corrected']['n']:,} and the same {sf['pilot_corrected']['events']} changes ({F(sf['pilot_corrected']['pct'])}%), and risks for shared participants agreed. The finalization and 2001–2002 protocols were saved on 2026-10-04 before the new calculations; no preregistration is claimed. pyprevent[[pyprevent]] risks agreed with independently sourced preventr coefficients[[preventr]] to within 2.85 × 10⁻¹⁴ pp in the primary, alternative-pressure and 2001–2002 runs and 4.26 × 10⁻¹⁴ pp in the blood pressure benchmark, and survey estimates and standard errors agreed with samplics 0.4.55 to within 10⁻¹⁶ (3.4 × 10⁻¹⁶ for the post hoc flag and selection estimates). SAS transport files were read with pyreadstat 1.3.6 because pandas 3.0.1 represented some true zeros as tiny positive values.")
    h2('Post hoc analyses')
    p('These were specified after the primary and 2001–2002 results were known. The flag, selection and fasting analyses reuse saved predictions without rescoring; the blood pressure benchmark rescored PREVENT. Workload is the weighted number selected divided by the weighted cohort; coverage is the weighted number of selected participants who changed category divided by the weighted number of all changes; and yield is the weighted number of selected changes divided by the weighted number selected. Values of 100% when everyone is selected are identities without intervals. The 0.5 pp window was inclusive on both sides of each threshold and counted overlapping selections once; selection on the second specimen is retrospective, because that result is not available at the first visit. In the fasting subset (fasting-subsample weights), crossing in either direction counts participants whose two risks lie on different sides of a threshold, and net change is the weighted mean of the indicator that the second risk is at or above the threshold minus that for the first, with a linear t interval. The blood pressure benchmark was specified in a protocol saved before its calculation: risk was recalculated with a single SBP reading in place of the mean (reading 1 replaced by reading 2; secondarily reading 2 by reading 3), both readings within 90–200 mm Hg and UACR held at the random value; rescoring with the mean reproduced every saved risk.')
    return b

